1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,461
Could we get more out of a quantum computer by asking it to do less?

2
00:00:04,481 --> 00:00:08,877
That is the question behind Google Quantum AI's Kaggle competition:

3
00:00:08,897 --> 00:00:11,447
Verifiable Quantum Circuit Compilation.

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:16,413
The prize pool is one hundred and fifty thousand dollars,

5
00:00:16,433 --> 00:00:21,639
and the organizers say you don't need a quantum computing background to take part.

6
00:00:21,659 --> 00:00:23,997
But you do need a working vocabulary.

7
00:00:24,017 --> 00:00:29,160
Words like Toffoli gate, uncomputation, phase kickback, syndrome, block encoding.

8
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:33,303
Without them, the discussion forum reads like a foreign language.

9
00:00:33,667 --> 00:00:37,748
This video builds that vocabulary from the ground up, with pictures.

10
00:00:37,768 --> 00:00:42,513
By the end, you should be able to read the competition paper, follow the forum,

11
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:44,986
and start designing circuits of your own.

12
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:48,997
Here is the whole competition in one picture.

13
00:00:49,017 --> 00:00:53,793
Future error-corrected quantum computers will run enormous algorithms.

14
00:00:53,813 --> 00:00:59,343
Inside each algorithm there is usually one subroutine that eats most of the cost.

15
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:04,424
Your job is to rewrite that subroutine as a cheaper circuit.

16
00:01:04,444 --> 00:01:08,666
Fewer qubits, and fewer of the expensive gates called Toffoli gates.

17
00:01:08,686 --> 00:01:11,349
But it must still compute the right answer,

18
00:01:11,369 --> 00:01:14,718
and a program called the verifier checks that it does.

19
00:01:15,867 --> 00:01:19,937
There are three such subroutines, each taken from a real application.

20
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:20,551
One:

21
00:01:20,571 --> 00:01:23,333
phasing by one over the square root of x.

22
00:01:23,353 --> 00:01:28,420
That's the bottleneck when simulating the electrons inside a fusion plasma.

23
00:01:28,767 --> 00:01:29,030
Two:

24
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:34,698
decoding a Reed–Solomon code, the bottleneck of an optimization algorithm called

25
00:01:34,718 --> 00:01:36,894
decoded quantum interferometry.

26
00:01:37,233 --> 00:01:37,877
And three:

27
00:01:37,897 --> 00:01:40,994
looking up stored angles and rotating orbitals.

28
00:01:41,014 --> 00:01:43,845
That's the bottleneck of simulating FeMoCo,

29
00:01:43,865 --> 00:01:49,151
the cluster inside the enzyme that lets bacteria turn nitrogen from the air into

30
00:01:49,171 --> 00:01:49,881
fertilizer.

31
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:55,642
Each benchmark ships with a reference circuit from published research,

32
00:01:55,662 --> 00:01:56,844
and a cost formula.

33
00:01:56,864 --> 00:02:00,893
Your ratio on a task is the reference cost divided by your cost.

34
00:02:00,913 --> 00:02:03,171
Match the reference and you get one.

35
00:02:03,191 --> 00:02:06,334
Build a circuit ten times cheaper and you get ten.

36
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:10,787
The leaderboard score is the geometric mean of your three ratios.

37
00:02:10,807 --> 00:02:15,906
Because it's a product, doubling your ratio on any one task helps exactly as much

38
00:02:15,926 --> 00:02:17,675
as doubling it on any other.

39
00:02:17,695 --> 00:02:21,656
You can't ignore a task, but you can attack them one at a time.

40
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:24,808
Now here is the surprising part.

41
00:02:24,828 --> 00:02:30,067
Checking whether a general quantum circuit is correct is believed to be intractable

42
00:02:30,087 --> 00:02:33,299
for classical computers, beyond about forty qubits.

43
00:02:33,667 --> 00:02:38,354
Yet this competition's verifier checks circuits with hundreds of qubits and

44
00:02:38,374 --> 00:02:40,676
millions of gates on an ordinary CPU.

45
00:02:40,696 --> 00:02:44,818
The reason is a special kind of circuit, called a kickmix circuit.

46
00:02:44,838 --> 00:02:49,400
Understanding why kickmix circuits can be checked is the key to the whole

47
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:50,153
competition.

48
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:52,242
Here's the plan.

49
00:02:52,262 --> 00:02:55,007
First, the minimum quantum computing you need:

50
00:02:55,027 --> 00:02:58,613
qubits, gates, and why some gates cost far more than others.

51
00:02:58,633 --> 00:03:01,498
Then kickmix circuits, and how they're verified.

52
00:03:01,518 --> 00:03:05,405
Then the standard toolkit of tricks that every top solution uses.

53
00:03:05,425 --> 00:03:08,050
Then each of the three benchmarks in detail.

54
00:03:08,070 --> 00:03:08,771
And finally:

55
00:03:08,791 --> 00:03:12,978
how the scoring works, how to submit, and where the points are hiding.

56
00:03:17,533 --> 00:03:20,480
Let's start with what a quantum computer stores:

57
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:20,913
qubits.

58
00:03:20,933 --> 00:03:24,251
And to understand a qubit, start with an ordinary bit.

59
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:26,575
A bit is either zero or one.

60
00:03:26,595 --> 00:03:31,635
A register of n bits holds exactly one of two to the n possible values.

61
00:03:31,655 --> 00:03:34,200
Three bits hold one of eight values:

62
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:37,977
for example one, zero, one, which is the number five.

63
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:41,345
A qubit is described differently.

64
00:03:41,365 --> 00:03:44,659
Its state is a pair of numbers called amplitudes:

65
00:03:44,679 --> 00:03:46,688
one for zero, and one for one.

66
00:03:46,708 --> 00:03:48,175
We write it like this:

67
00:03:48,195 --> 00:03:50,610
alpha, ket zero, plus beta, ket one.

68
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:55,885
The angled bracket, called a ket, is just a label for one of the basis states.

69
00:03:56,233 --> 00:03:59,136
Picture the state as an arrow of length one.

70
00:03:59,156 --> 00:04:02,525
Pointing along the horizontal axis, it is ket zero.

71
00:04:02,545 --> 00:04:04,916
Pointing straight up, it is ket one.

72
00:04:04,936 --> 00:04:07,706
Anywhere in between, it's a superposition:

73
00:04:07,726 --> 00:04:08,636
a bit of each.

74
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,367
When you measure a qubit, you never see the amplitudes.

75
00:04:12,387 --> 00:04:15,631
You see zero, with probability alpha squared, or one,

76
00:04:15,651 --> 00:04:20,251
with probability beta squared, and the state collapses to whatever you saw.

77
00:04:20,271 --> 00:04:22,468
That's why the arrow has length one:

78
00:04:22,488 --> 00:04:24,993
the two probabilities must add up to one.

79
00:04:25,333 --> 00:04:29,885
Measure many copies of this state and roughly a quarter of the results are zero,

80
00:04:29,905 --> 00:04:31,428
and three quarters are one.

81
00:04:32,567 --> 00:04:36,644
Amplitudes can be negative, and in general they're complex numbers.

82
00:04:36,664 --> 00:04:40,007
That's the real difference from ordinary probabilities.

83
00:04:40,027 --> 00:04:43,920
Two ways of reaching the same outcome can cancel each other out.

84
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:48,629
This is called interference, and it's where every quantum speedup comes from.

85
00:04:49,767 --> 00:04:52,378
Amplitudes are also where phases live.

86
00:04:52,398 --> 00:04:56,395
A phase is a complex number of size one, e to the i theta,

87
00:04:56,415 --> 00:04:58,265
a point on the unit circle.

88
00:04:58,285 --> 00:05:03,528
Multiplying a whole state by a phase changes nothing you could ever measure.

89
00:05:03,867 --> 00:05:08,366
But a phase on one part of a superposition, relative to another part,

90
00:05:08,386 --> 00:05:11,182
changes how those parts interfere later on.

91
00:05:11,202 --> 00:05:12,361
Keep this in mind.

92
00:05:12,381 --> 00:05:17,797
Two of the three benchmarks are about putting exactly the right phase on each basis

93
00:05:17,817 --> 00:05:18,190
state.

94
00:05:19,333 --> 00:05:20,923
Now take several qubits.

95
00:05:20,943 --> 00:05:26,154
With n qubits there are two to the n basis states, one for every n-bit string,

96
00:05:26,174 --> 00:05:30,112
and a general state has an amplitude for every one of them.

97
00:05:30,132 --> 00:05:30,983
Three qubits:

98
00:05:31,003 --> 00:05:32,124
eight amplitudes.

99
00:05:32,467 --> 00:05:33,318
Fifty qubits:

100
00:05:33,338 --> 00:05:35,663
about a million billion amplitudes.

101
00:05:35,683 --> 00:05:37,070
A few hundred qubits:

102
00:05:37,090 --> 00:05:40,421
more numbers than there are atoms in the universe.

103
00:05:40,441 --> 00:05:45,380
That's why simulating a quantum computer in general is exponentially hard.

104
00:05:46,533 --> 00:05:47,911
But notice something.

105
00:05:47,931 --> 00:05:52,038
If the state is a single basis state, like ket one, zero, one,

106
00:05:52,058 --> 00:05:54,434
then only one amplitude is non-zero.

107
00:05:54,454 --> 00:05:59,759
You can describe the whole state with three bits, plus one number for its phase.

108
00:05:59,779 --> 00:06:01,156
Hold on to this idea.

109
00:06:01,176 --> 00:06:04,218
It's the seed of everything the verifier does.

110
00:06:05,367 --> 00:06:06,304
One last idea:

111
00:06:06,324 --> 00:06:07,193
entanglement.

112
00:06:07,213 --> 00:06:11,912
A two-qubit state like ket zero zero plus ket one one, over root two,

113
00:06:11,932 --> 00:06:15,468
can't be split into a separate state for each qubit.

114
00:06:15,488 --> 00:06:19,024
Measure one qubit, and you instantly know the other.

115
00:06:19,367 --> 00:06:23,309
In this competition you'll meet entanglement in a specific form.

116
00:06:23,329 --> 00:06:27,023
A circuit computes some function f of x into scratch qubits,

117
00:06:27,043 --> 00:06:29,685
which entangles the scratch with the input.

118
00:06:29,705 --> 00:06:33,399
Before it finishes, the circuit must disentangle them again.

119
00:06:33,419 --> 00:06:37,237
That's called uncomputation, and it's where a lot of the cost,

120
00:06:37,257 --> 00:06:39,404
and a lot of the cleverness, lives.

121
00:06:43,967 --> 00:06:49,623
A quantum computation is a sequence of gates applied to qubits, drawn as a circuit.

122
00:06:49,643 --> 00:06:51,949
Each horizontal line is one qubit.

123
00:06:51,969 --> 00:06:54,000
Time flows from left to right,

124
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:57,899
and each symbol on a line is a gate acting on that qubit.

125
00:06:59,067 --> 00:07:01,906
The simplest gate is X, the quantum NOT.

126
00:07:01,926 --> 00:07:06,051
It turns ket zero into ket one, and ket one into ket zero.

127
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:09,793
On a superposition, it simply swaps the two amplitudes.

128
00:07:10,933 --> 00:07:13,263
Next, the controlled NOT, or CNOT.

129
00:07:13,283 --> 00:07:17,892
The dot is the control, and the circle with the plus is the target.

130
00:07:17,912 --> 00:07:20,656
If the control is one, the target flips.

131
00:07:20,676 --> 00:07:22,521
Otherwise, nothing happens.

132
00:07:22,541 --> 00:07:23,627
Put another way:

133
00:07:23,647 --> 00:07:26,252
the target becomes target XOR control.

134
00:07:27,400 --> 00:07:30,310
Then the Toffoli gate, also called C C X:

135
00:07:30,330 --> 00:07:32,311
two controls and one target.

136
00:07:32,331 --> 00:07:35,812
The target flips only when both controls are one.

137
00:07:36,167 --> 00:07:41,128
If the target starts at zero, the Toffoli computes the AND of the two controls.

138
00:07:41,148 --> 00:07:42,326
Remember this gate.

139
00:07:42,346 --> 00:07:45,038
It's the currency of the whole competition:

140
00:07:45,058 --> 00:07:49,011
most of your score is the number of Toffolis your circuit uses.

141
00:07:50,167 --> 00:07:52,293
These gates permute basis states.

142
00:07:52,313 --> 00:07:56,196
Each input bit string goes to exactly one output bit string,

143
00:07:56,216 --> 00:07:59,968
and no two inputs collide, so no information is ever lost.

144
00:07:59,988 --> 00:08:02,180
That's what makes them reversible:

145
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:05,497
run the circuit backwards, and you undo it exactly.

146
00:08:05,517 --> 00:08:07,513
Quantum mechanics demands this.

147
00:08:07,533 --> 00:08:11,091
Every gate, apart from measurement, must be reversible.

148
00:08:12,233 --> 00:08:17,381
With X, CNOT and Toffoli you can build any classical computation reversibly:

149
00:08:17,401 --> 00:08:22,481
adders, multipliers, comparisons, even a complete error-correction decoder.

150
00:08:22,501 --> 00:08:25,269
The trick is never to overwrite anything.

151
00:08:25,289 --> 00:08:29,349
Each result is written into fresh qubits that start at zero.

152
00:08:29,369 --> 00:08:33,293
Those extra qubits are called ancillas, or scratch qubits.

153
00:08:34,433 --> 00:08:38,641
There's a catch, and it's the central theme of this competition.

154
00:08:38,661 --> 00:08:41,812
Scratch qubits fill up with intermediate values:

155
00:08:41,832 --> 00:08:42,340
garbage.

156
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:45,115
If garbage stays entangled with your data,

157
00:08:45,135 --> 00:08:48,351
it ruins the interference later in the algorithm.

158
00:08:48,371 --> 00:08:51,522
So every scratch qubit must be returned to zero.

159
00:08:51,542 --> 00:08:52,909
That's uncomputation.

160
00:08:53,267 --> 00:08:56,075
The classic recipe, due to Charles Bennett:

161
00:08:56,095 --> 00:09:01,073
compute, copy the answer out with CNOTs, then run the computation backwards.

162
00:09:01,093 --> 00:09:02,915
The scratch returns to zero.

163
00:09:02,935 --> 00:09:05,480
But you paid for the computation twice.

164
00:09:06,633 --> 00:09:08,336
Now the second family:

165
00:09:08,356 --> 00:09:09,276
phase gates.

166
00:09:09,296 --> 00:09:14,759
The Z gate leaves ket zero alone, and multiplies ket one by minus one.

167
00:09:14,779 --> 00:09:17,814
It changes no bits at all, only a sign.

168
00:09:17,834 --> 00:09:22,827
The controlled Z, or C Z, puts a minus sign only on ket one one.

169
00:09:22,847 --> 00:09:25,568
And C C Z, only on ket one one one.

170
00:09:25,933 --> 00:09:31,605
More generally, Z to the power t multiplies ket one by e to the i pi t.

171
00:09:31,625 --> 00:09:35,453
In kickmix this gate is called Z underscore POW.

172
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:38,407
For t equal to one, it's Z.

173
00:09:38,427 --> 00:09:40,911
For one half, it's called the S gate.

174
00:09:40,931 --> 00:09:42,805
For one quarter, the T gate.

175
00:09:42,825 --> 00:09:45,377
Any other angle is a general rotation.

176
00:09:45,397 --> 00:09:50,046
As we'll see in the next chapter, these have wildly different prices.

177
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:52,728
Here's the key property.

178
00:09:52,748 --> 00:09:54,340
Phase gates are diagonal:

179
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:57,371
each basis state goes to itself, times a phase.

180
00:09:57,391 --> 00:10:00,595
Permutation gates move one basis state to another.

181
00:10:00,615 --> 00:10:05,883
So a circuit built only from these two kinds of gates sends every basis state to a

182
00:10:05,903 --> 00:10:08,076
single basis state, times a phase.

183
00:10:08,096 --> 00:10:10,333
Remember the seed from chapter one?

184
00:10:10,353 --> 00:10:13,686
This is the property the whole verifier is built on.

185
00:10:14,833 --> 00:10:15,756
One more gate:

186
00:10:15,776 --> 00:10:16,833
the Hadamard, H.

187
00:10:16,853 --> 00:10:19,862
This is the gate that creates superpositions.

188
00:10:19,882 --> 00:10:23,363
It turns ket zero into an equal mix of zero and one.

189
00:10:23,383 --> 00:10:27,470
Hadamards, and the quantum Fourier transform built from them,

190
00:10:27,490 --> 00:10:30,634
are where interference happens in an algorithm.

191
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,832
But those parts of the algorithm aren't in this competition.

192
00:10:34,852 --> 00:10:39,455
Inside the competition's circuits, H only appears in one disguised form,

193
00:10:39,475 --> 00:10:41,574
as part of a special measurement.

194
00:10:41,594 --> 00:10:43,500
We'll meet it in chapter four.

195
00:10:44,667 --> 00:10:50,410
Put it together, and you get the pattern you'll see everywhere in this competition:

196
00:10:50,430 --> 00:10:52,216
compute, phase, uncompute.

197
00:10:52,236 --> 00:10:57,980
To multiply each basis state ket x by a phase that depends on some function f of x:

198
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,410
compute f of x into scratch qubits.

199
00:11:00,430 --> 00:11:03,188
Apply phase gates to the bits of f of x.

200
00:11:03,208 --> 00:11:04,368
Then uncompute f.

201
00:11:04,733 --> 00:11:09,120
The scratch returns to zero, and only the phase is left behind.

202
00:11:09,140 --> 00:11:11,777
Benchmark one is exactly this pattern,

203
00:11:11,797 --> 00:11:15,344
with f of x equal to one over the square root of x.

204
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:25,485
Why does the competition count Toffoli gates, rather than all gates equally?

205
00:11:25,505 --> 00:11:27,460
Because real qubits are noisy.

206
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:32,860
Today's best physical qubits make an error roughly once every thousand operations,

207
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:37,272
and the algorithms in this competition need billions of operations.

208
00:11:38,133 --> 00:11:40,513
The fix is quantum error correction.

209
00:11:40,533 --> 00:11:44,111
Many physical qubits, hundreds up to a thousand or so,

210
00:11:44,131 --> 00:11:48,643
are woven together into one logical qubit that is far more reliable,

211
00:11:48,663 --> 00:11:51,309
for example a patch of the surface code.

212
00:11:51,329 --> 00:11:55,041
Algorithms, including every circuit in this competition,

213
00:11:55,061 --> 00:11:57,640
are written in terms of logical qubits.

214
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:02,164
Error-corrected computers can do some gates cheaply.

215
00:12:02,184 --> 00:12:04,051
These are the Clifford gates:

216
00:12:04,071 --> 00:12:05,613
X, Z, H, S, CNOT and CZ.

217
00:12:05,633 --> 00:12:08,282
On the surface code, they're nearly free.

218
00:12:08,302 --> 00:12:10,820
But Clifford gates alone aren't enough:

219
00:12:10,840 --> 00:12:15,701
circuits made only of Cliffords can be simulated efficiently on an ordinary

220
00:12:15,721 --> 00:12:19,020
computer, so they can't give any quantum advantage.

221
00:12:19,367 --> 00:12:23,262
To go beyond, you need a non-Clifford gate, like T or Toffoli.

222
00:12:23,282 --> 00:12:26,734
These need special resource states called magic states,

223
00:12:26,754 --> 00:12:31,849
which are produced in factories that take up a big chunk of the machine and a lot

224
00:12:31,869 --> 00:12:32,354
of time.

225
00:12:32,374 --> 00:12:37,658
So the number of non-Clifford gates largely sets the runtime of the whole algorithm.

226
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:40,605
You can picture it like this.

227
00:12:40,625 --> 00:12:43,311
The computation runs Clifford gates freely,

228
00:12:43,331 --> 00:12:48,030
but every Toffoli has to wait for a magic state to arrive from the factory.

229
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:52,685
So here's the cost model this competition uses.

230
00:12:52,705 --> 00:12:54,848
Count the Toffoli-like gates:

231
00:12:54,868 --> 00:12:57,683
C C X, C C Z, and the controlled swap.

232
00:12:57,703 --> 00:13:01,113
Count T gates, at a quarter of a Toffoli each.

233
00:13:01,467 --> 00:13:05,432
Arbitrary rotations have to be built out of many T gates,

234
00:13:05,452 --> 00:13:09,696
and the more precise the rotation, the more T gates it takes.

235
00:13:09,716 --> 00:13:15,289
In benchmark one, a rotation with error epsilon is priced at zero point five six

236
00:13:15,309 --> 00:13:20,253
times log base two of one over epsilon, plus five point three, T gates.

237
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:24,211
Clifford gates, measurement and classical logic are free.

238
00:13:24,231 --> 00:13:29,371
And qubits are counted too, because each logical qubit costs hundreds of physical

239
00:13:29,391 --> 00:13:29,689
ones.

240
00:13:30,833 --> 00:13:35,882
For example, a rotation accurate to one part in a billion, about thirty bits,

241
00:13:35,902 --> 00:13:37,989
costs around twenty two T gates.

242
00:13:38,009 --> 00:13:42,070
That's five and a half Toffolis' worth, for a single rotation.

243
00:13:42,090 --> 00:13:47,402
So fewer rotations, and looser precision where you can afford it, both save cost.

244
00:13:48,567 --> 00:13:51,633
Each benchmark combines these into one number.

245
00:13:51,653 --> 00:13:52,773
Roughly speaking:

246
00:13:52,793 --> 00:13:54,249
qubits times Toffolis.

247
00:13:54,269 --> 00:13:56,597
Think of it as a space-time volume.

248
00:13:56,617 --> 00:14:00,488
How much machine you need, times how long you need it for.

249
00:14:00,833 --> 00:14:04,806
Halve the qubits, or halve the Toffolis, and the cost halves either way.

250
00:14:05,967 --> 00:14:07,566
And notice what's free:

251
00:14:07,586 --> 00:14:11,578
X, CNOT, Z, C Z, swaps, measurement, and classical logic.

252
00:14:11,598 --> 00:14:16,998
Any trick that replaces a Toffoli with a handful of free gates is a pure win.

253
00:14:17,018 --> 00:14:22,630
And as you're about to see, measurement is the most valuable free tool you have.

254
00:14:27,766 --> 00:14:30,472
Now we can define kickmix circuits.

255
00:14:30,492 --> 00:14:35,146
A kickmix circuit is allowed only four kinds of instruction.

256
00:14:35,166 --> 00:14:37,249
Reversible classical gates:

257
00:14:37,269 --> 00:14:39,275
X, CNOT, Toffoli and swap.

258
00:14:39,295 --> 00:14:40,910
Diagonal phase gates:

259
00:14:40,930 --> 00:14:45,584
Z, C Z, C C Z, Z POW, and NEG, which flips the overall sign.

260
00:14:45,933 --> 00:14:47,367
Measurement and reset:

261
00:14:47,387 --> 00:14:48,028
HMR and R.

262
00:14:48,048 --> 00:14:52,392
And operations on classical bits, which store measurement results.

263
00:14:52,412 --> 00:14:56,955
On top of that, any instruction can be conditioned on classical bits,

264
00:14:56,975 --> 00:14:57,946
so you can say:

265
00:14:57,966 --> 00:15:00,723
do this only if that measurement gave one.

266
00:15:01,866 --> 00:15:04,226
Feed a kickmix circuit a basis state,

267
00:15:04,246 --> 00:15:08,663
and every instruction maps it to a single basis state, times a phase.

268
00:15:08,683 --> 00:15:12,522
So to simulate it, you only need to store one bit per qubit,

269
00:15:12,542 --> 00:15:14,452
plus one number for the phase.

270
00:15:14,799 --> 00:15:18,556
Simulating a gate is just flipping a bit, or flipping the sign.

271
00:15:18,576 --> 00:15:21,493
The time grows linearly with the number of gates,

272
00:15:21,513 --> 00:15:24,131
not exponentially with the number of qubits.

273
00:15:25,299 --> 00:15:26,624
And kickmix is fast.

274
00:15:26,644 --> 00:15:32,135
It's a C plus plus library with Python bindings, inside Google's Qualtran package.

275
00:15:32,155 --> 00:15:37,714
It packs between sixty four and five hundred twelve independent runs, called shots,

276
00:15:37,734 --> 00:15:39,865
into the CPU's vector registers,

277
00:15:39,885 --> 00:15:45,242
and simulates tens of billions of gate applications per second on a single core.

278
00:15:46,399 --> 00:15:50,477
The most interesting instruction is HMR, pronounced hammer:

279
00:15:50,497 --> 00:15:52,214
Hadamard, measure, reset.

280
00:15:52,234 --> 00:15:56,312
It measures a qubit in the X basis, records a random bit b,

281
00:15:56,332 --> 00:15:58,326
and resets the qubit to zero.

282
00:15:58,666 --> 00:16:00,147
Here's why it matters.

283
00:16:00,167 --> 00:16:04,856
Suppose a scratch qubit holds a function of the other qubits, f of x.

284
00:16:04,876 --> 00:16:05,539
Hammer it.

285
00:16:05,559 --> 00:16:09,019
The qubit comes back as zero, cleanly disentangled.

286
00:16:09,039 --> 00:16:13,660
In exchange, you get a random bit b, and the state picks up a phase:

287
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:16,253
minus one to the power b times f of x.

288
00:16:16,599 --> 00:16:18,624
If b is zero, nothing happened.

289
00:16:18,644 --> 00:16:19,416
You're done.

290
00:16:19,436 --> 00:16:24,429
If b is one, there's a wrong sign on exactly the states where f of x is one.

291
00:16:24,449 --> 00:16:25,419
But you know b.

292
00:16:25,439 --> 00:16:26,804
It's a classical bit.

293
00:16:26,824 --> 00:16:32,213
So you can undo the sign with a phase gate on the qubits that f was computed from,

294
00:16:32,233 --> 00:16:33,335
conditioned on b.

295
00:16:34,499 --> 00:16:37,217
The classic example is the temporary AND.

296
00:16:37,237 --> 00:16:40,888
Computing a and b into a fresh qubit costs one Toffoli.

297
00:16:40,908 --> 00:16:42,958
You use the result for a while.

298
00:16:43,299 --> 00:16:47,601
Uncomputing it the textbook way, by running the Toffoli again,

299
00:16:47,621 --> 00:16:49,205
costs a second Toffoli.

300
00:16:49,225 --> 00:16:49,762
Instead:

301
00:16:49,782 --> 00:16:50,459
hammer it.

302
00:16:50,479 --> 00:16:54,503
If the measurement gives one, apply a C Z between a and b.

303
00:16:54,523 --> 00:16:59,313
That cancels the stray sign exactly, because the sign was on a AND b.

304
00:16:59,666 --> 00:17:01,243
A C Z is a Clifford gate.

305
00:17:01,263 --> 00:17:01,881
It's free.

306
00:17:01,901 --> 00:17:06,288
You've just halved the cost of every temporary value in your circuit.

307
00:17:06,308 --> 00:17:11,333
This trick is due to Craig Gidney, one of the authors of the competition paper,

308
00:17:11,353 --> 00:17:12,610
and it's everywhere:

309
00:17:12,630 --> 00:17:15,995
in the reference circuits, and in every top solution.

310
00:17:17,133 --> 00:17:20,059
What if you hammer a qubit and forget the fix-up?

311
00:17:20,079 --> 00:17:23,787
Then the phase is wrong whenever the random bit comes out one,

312
00:17:23,807 --> 00:17:25,170
which is half the time.

313
00:17:25,190 --> 00:17:27,635
The simulator draws those bits at random,

314
00:17:27,655 --> 00:17:32,025
so the bug shows up in about half the shots, and the verifier catches it.

315
00:17:32,366 --> 00:17:35,820
The plain reset instruction, R, works the same way.

316
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:39,840
Resetting a qubit that isn't zero gives a random sign flip.

317
00:17:39,860 --> 00:17:42,360
Garbage can't be quietly thrown away.

318
00:17:42,380 --> 00:17:45,562
Every bit of it has to be genuinely uncomputed.

319
00:17:46,699 --> 00:17:49,433
This is measurement-based uncomputation.

320
00:17:49,453 --> 00:17:50,741
The general lesson:

321
00:17:50,761 --> 00:17:53,013
computing a value costs Toffolis.

322
00:17:53,033 --> 00:17:58,451
Erasing a value, by measuring it and fixing up the phase, is often nearly free,

323
00:17:58,471 --> 00:18:01,067
as long as the fix-up itself is cheap.

324
00:18:01,433 --> 00:18:04,196
One subtle point that matters for scoring.

325
00:18:04,216 --> 00:18:08,438
The verifier counts the Toffolis actually executed in each shot,

326
00:18:08,458 --> 00:18:10,095
then averages over shots.

327
00:18:10,115 --> 00:18:15,397
A Toffoli that's conditioned on a measured bit only counts on the shots where it

328
00:18:15,417 --> 00:18:15,729
runs.

329
00:18:15,749 --> 00:18:19,440
So clever classical control can lower your average cost.

330
00:18:24,598 --> 00:18:28,241
So how does the verifier decide that a circuit is correct?

331
00:18:28,261 --> 00:18:33,419
It runs the circuit on classical inputs, and on every shot it checks three things.

332
00:18:33,439 --> 00:18:36,388
The input registers hold their original values.

333
00:18:36,408 --> 00:18:38,661
Every scratch qubit is back to zero.

334
00:18:38,681 --> 00:18:43,650
And the accumulated phase matches the target, within the benchmark's tolerance.

335
00:18:44,798 --> 00:18:49,856
When the set of possible inputs is small enough, you can simply test all of them.

336
00:18:49,876 --> 00:18:52,739
In benchmark one, that's exactly what happens:

337
00:18:52,759 --> 00:18:57,190
every one of the three point one million inputs, sixty four times each.

338
00:18:57,210 --> 00:18:59,259
That's two hundred million shots.

339
00:18:59,598 --> 00:19:02,376
When the input space is astronomically large,

340
00:19:02,396 --> 00:19:06,479
like the eight hundred and forty qubit syndromes of benchmark two,

341
00:19:06,499 --> 00:19:09,339
the verifier samples inputs at random instead.

342
00:19:09,359 --> 00:19:11,018
That's called fuzz testing.

343
00:19:11,038 --> 00:19:13,567
It can't prove that a circuit is perfect,

344
00:19:13,587 --> 00:19:16,364
but it can put a bound on how often it fails.

345
00:19:17,531 --> 00:19:19,879
Why run each input sixty four times?

346
00:19:19,899 --> 00:19:22,445
Because the hammer outcomes are random.

347
00:19:22,465 --> 00:19:25,602
A missing fix-up only shows up on some outcomes.

348
00:19:25,622 --> 00:19:30,799
A bug that strikes with probability one half per shot slips past all sixty four

349
00:19:30,819 --> 00:19:34,153
shots with probability two to the minus sixty four.

350
00:19:34,173 --> 00:19:35,798
That's essentially never.

351
00:19:36,965 --> 00:19:39,143
But who picks the random test inputs?

352
00:19:39,163 --> 00:19:41,222
If the tests were fixed and public,

353
00:19:41,242 --> 00:19:44,845
someone could hard-code the answers for exactly those inputs.

354
00:19:45,198 --> 00:19:48,123
The trick is called the Fiat Shamir heuristic.

355
00:19:48,143 --> 00:19:52,988
The verifier takes the bytes of your circuit file, hashes them with SHA-256,

356
00:19:53,008 --> 00:19:58,110
and uses the hash as the seed of the random number generator that picks the test

357
00:19:58,130 --> 00:20:00,415
inputs and the measurement outcomes.

358
00:20:00,765 --> 00:20:04,971
Change a single byte of your circuit, and you get a completely different hash,

359
00:20:04,991 --> 00:20:07,302
and so a completely different set of tests.

360
00:20:07,322 --> 00:20:10,715
You can't know the tests until you've committed to the circuit.

361
00:20:11,065 --> 00:20:12,119
One more defence.

362
00:20:12,139 --> 00:20:16,730
Without it, you could add do-nothing gates to your circuit over and over,

363
00:20:16,750 --> 00:20:19,447
until the hash happened to pick easy tests.

364
00:20:19,467 --> 00:20:21,215
That's called seed grinding.

365
00:20:21,235 --> 00:20:24,563
So the competition mixes a secret salt into the hash,

366
00:20:24,583 --> 00:20:27,785
which is only revealed after submissions are fixed.

367
00:20:28,931 --> 00:20:34,141
From the results, the verifier computes an upper bound on the true failure rate:

368
00:20:34,161 --> 00:20:36,364
a one-sided Clopper Pearson bound,

369
00:20:36,384 --> 00:20:40,940
at confidence one minus two to the minus one hundred and twenty eight.

370
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:44,274
If a circuit fails on a fraction epsilon of inputs,

371
00:20:44,294 --> 00:20:49,112
the chance that it passes N shots in a row is one minus epsilon, to the N.

372
00:20:49,465 --> 00:20:54,290
With zero failures, that gives a bound of about eighty nine divided by N.

373
00:20:54,310 --> 00:20:59,402
So a hundred thousand clean shots certify a failure rate below about one in a

374
00:20:59,422 --> 00:20:59,999
thousand.

375
00:21:01,165 --> 00:21:05,779
Why does passing on classical inputs tell you anything about superpositions,

376
00:21:05,799 --> 00:21:08,340
which is what the real algorithm feeds in?

377
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:09,560
The paper proves it.

378
00:21:09,580 --> 00:21:12,791
If the circuit fails on a fraction epsilon of inputs,

379
00:21:12,811 --> 00:21:17,243
with inputs drawn from the distribution the real algorithm would produce,

380
00:21:17,263 --> 00:21:21,816
then the output state is within distance two root epsilon of the ideal one.

381
00:21:21,836 --> 00:21:26,450
That's why each benchmark carefully specifies how its test inputs are drawn.

382
00:21:26,798 --> 00:21:28,714
And that has a big consequence:

383
00:21:28,734 --> 00:21:30,775
approximate circuits are allowed.

384
00:21:30,795 --> 00:21:35,833
Dropping a rarely needed carry bit, truncating a register, stopping a loop early:

385
00:21:35,853 --> 00:21:39,081
all legal, as long as the errors stay within budget.

386
00:21:39,101 --> 00:21:43,764
In benchmark one, every shot must pass, but the phase only has to be close.

387
00:21:43,784 --> 00:21:48,385
In benchmark two, occasional failures are allowed, and cost you a penalty.

388
00:21:53,531 --> 00:21:56,822
Before we get to the benchmarks, let's fill the toolbox.

389
00:21:56,842 --> 00:22:01,020
These are the building blocks you'll find in kickmix's builder library,

390
00:22:01,040 --> 00:22:04,213
and in nearly every forum post about this competition.

391
00:22:05,365 --> 00:22:05,916
Addition.

392
00:22:05,936 --> 00:22:10,420
To add a into b, you ripple a carry from the lowest bit to the highest.

393
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:13,465
Each carry is a majority function of three bits,

394
00:22:13,485 --> 00:22:17,398
and with the temporary AND trick it costs one Toffoli per bit.

395
00:22:17,765 --> 00:22:23,147
Then you sweep back down, writing the sum and erasing each carry by measurement,

396
00:22:23,167 --> 00:22:24,430
at no Toffoli cost.

397
00:22:24,450 --> 00:22:27,874
So adding two n-bit numbers costs about n Toffolis.

398
00:22:27,894 --> 00:22:30,980
Comparing and subtracting cost about the same.

399
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:34,154
This construction is from Craig Gidney's paper,

400
00:22:34,174 --> 00:22:36,652
halving the cost of quantum addition.

401
00:22:36,672 --> 00:22:37,935
It's worth reading.

402
00:22:39,098 --> 00:22:44,663
Multiplication of two n-bit numbers is basically n additions of shifted partial

403
00:22:44,683 --> 00:22:45,300
products:

404
00:22:45,320 --> 00:22:47,067
about n squared Toffolis.

405
00:22:47,087 --> 00:22:51,521
That's why big multiplications dominate any arithmetic circuit.

406
00:22:51,865 --> 00:22:54,561
But you rarely need every bit of a product.

407
00:22:54,581 --> 00:22:59,742
In fixed-point arithmetic, you can drop the partial products that only affect bits

408
00:22:59,762 --> 00:23:03,785
you'll throw away anyway, as long as the error is accounted for.

409
00:23:03,805 --> 00:23:07,386
Truncated multipliers are one of the most common savings.

410
00:23:08,531 --> 00:23:10,426
Which brings us to fixed point.

411
00:23:10,446 --> 00:23:13,762
There are no floating-point numbers in these circuits.

412
00:23:13,782 --> 00:23:17,283
A number is just an integer with an implied binary point,

413
00:23:17,303 --> 00:23:21,853
and every design decision is about how many bits to keep in each register.

414
00:23:21,873 --> 00:23:25,807
Fewer bits mean fewer Toffolis and fewer qubits, but more error.

415
00:23:26,965 --> 00:23:27,413
Shifts.

416
00:23:27,433 --> 00:23:30,759
Multiplying by a known power of two costs nothing:

417
00:23:30,779 --> 00:23:33,702
you just relabel which wire means which bit.

418
00:23:33,722 --> 00:23:38,921
But shifting by an amount that's stored in a quantum register needs controlled

419
00:23:38,941 --> 00:23:43,337
swaps, a barrel shifter, and each controlled swap costs a Toffoli.

420
00:23:44,498 --> 00:23:48,295
Now the single most important tool in this competition:

421
00:23:48,315 --> 00:23:50,933
the quantum lookup table, called QROM.

422
00:23:50,953 --> 00:23:54,542
A table of classical data is baked into the circuit.

423
00:23:54,562 --> 00:23:57,041
Given an address register holding a,

424
00:23:57,061 --> 00:24:01,205
the lookup XORs the data word d of a into a target register.

425
00:24:01,565 --> 00:24:04,793
Because the address register can be in superposition,

426
00:24:04,813 --> 00:24:09,942
one lookup reads every entry at once, each into its own branch of the superposition.

427
00:24:11,098 --> 00:24:12,176
How does it work?

428
00:24:12,196 --> 00:24:14,760
With a technique called unary iteration.

429
00:24:14,780 --> 00:24:17,667
Picture a binary tree over all the addresses.

430
00:24:17,687 --> 00:24:22,965
Walking down the tree, each node is a temporary AND of its parent with one address

431
00:24:22,985 --> 00:24:23,223
bit.

432
00:24:23,565 --> 00:24:26,901
For any address, exactly one leaf ends up switched on:

433
00:24:26,921 --> 00:24:28,703
the one matching the address.

434
00:24:28,723 --> 00:24:32,804
With the right bookkeeping, the circuit visits every leaf in turn,

435
00:24:32,824 --> 00:24:35,042
spending about one Toffoli per leaf.

436
00:24:35,398 --> 00:24:40,185
At each leaf, the stored word is copied into the target with CNOTs controlled by

437
00:24:40,205 --> 00:24:40,786
that leaf.

438
00:24:40,806 --> 00:24:41,687
CNOTs are free.

439
00:24:41,707 --> 00:24:44,150
So the width of the words doesn't matter.

440
00:24:45,298 --> 00:24:48,534
So a lookup over L entries costs about L Toffolis,

441
00:24:48,554 --> 00:24:50,943
regardless of how wide the words are.

442
00:24:50,963 --> 00:24:56,218
A table of five hundred and twelve entries with ninety-bit words costs about five

443
00:24:56,238 --> 00:24:58,041
hundred and twelve Toffolis.

444
00:24:58,061 --> 00:25:00,711
That's why lookups are a favourite trick:

445
00:25:00,731 --> 00:25:05,139
whenever a table is small, replace arithmetic with precomputed data.

446
00:25:05,498 --> 00:25:08,395
And erasing a lookup's output is even cheaper:

447
00:25:08,415 --> 00:25:12,835
measure the target, then repair the phases with a much smaller lookup,

448
00:25:12,855 --> 00:25:14,927
for roughly the square root of L.

449
00:25:14,947 --> 00:25:19,240
There are also space-time trade-offs, known as select-swap networks,

450
00:25:19,260 --> 00:25:22,855
that spend extra qubits to cut the Toffoli count further.

451
00:25:23,998 --> 00:25:26,944
And the last tool is classical precomputation.

452
00:25:26,964 --> 00:25:30,169
Anything that doesn't depend on the quantum input:

453
00:25:30,189 --> 00:25:34,941
coefficients, tables, constants, even the structure of the circuit itself,

454
00:25:34,961 --> 00:25:37,134
can be computed offline, for free.

455
00:25:37,154 --> 00:25:42,164
A big part of every winning idea in this competition is moving work out of the

456
00:25:42,184 --> 00:25:45,388
quantum circuit, and into classical preprocessing.

457
00:25:50,531 --> 00:25:55,133
Benchmark one comes from simulating matter under extreme conditions,

458
00:25:55,153 --> 00:25:59,074
like the inside of an inertial confinement fusion capsule.

459
00:25:59,094 --> 00:26:03,492
A key quantity for designing such reactors is the stopping power:

460
00:26:03,512 --> 00:26:08,181
how quickly a fast particle loses energy as it ploughs through a hot,

461
00:26:08,201 --> 00:26:09,948
dense plasma of electrons.

462
00:26:11,098 --> 00:26:15,199
In this simulation, electrons live on a three-dimensional grid,

463
00:26:15,219 --> 00:26:18,994
and each electron gets a register holding its coordinates.

464
00:26:19,014 --> 00:26:20,760
The energy has three parts:

465
00:26:20,780 --> 00:26:23,442
kinetic energy, attraction to the nuclei,

466
00:26:23,462 --> 00:26:26,451
and repulsion between every pair of electrons.

467
00:26:26,471 --> 00:26:30,769
That last term is one over the distance between the two electrons.

468
00:26:31,131 --> 00:26:35,082
Time evolution is done in small steps, called Trotter steps.

469
00:26:35,102 --> 00:26:40,245
In each step, for every pair of electrons, the state is multiplied by a phase:

470
00:26:40,265 --> 00:26:45,076
e to the minus i, times a small time step, times one over their distance.

471
00:26:45,096 --> 00:26:49,709
With eta electrons, that's about eta squared pairs, every single step.

472
00:26:49,729 --> 00:26:51,165
That's the bottleneck.

473
00:26:52,331 --> 00:26:57,865
Squared distance is a sum of squared coordinate differences, so it's an integer.

474
00:26:57,885 --> 00:26:58,559
Call it x.

475
00:26:58,579 --> 00:27:00,503
Then the core subroutine is:

476
00:27:00,523 --> 00:27:03,350
given an integer x in a quantum register,

477
00:27:03,370 --> 00:27:07,931
multiply the state by e to the two pi i over the square root of x.

478
00:27:07,951 --> 00:27:09,736
Compute, phase, uncompute.

479
00:27:10,098 --> 00:27:10,722
Precisely:

480
00:27:10,742 --> 00:27:14,651
the circuit gets one named register, x, of twenty two qubits.

481
00:27:14,671 --> 00:27:18,130
It must work for every x from one up to three million,

482
00:27:18,150 --> 00:27:21,866
one hundred thirty three thousand, four hundred fifty two.

483
00:27:21,886 --> 00:27:26,697
That's the largest squared distance on a grid with ten bits per coordinate.

484
00:27:27,065 --> 00:27:30,951
The phase is measured in turns, fractions of a full circle,

485
00:27:30,971 --> 00:27:35,917
and it must be accurate to two to the minus twenty four, divided by root x.

486
00:27:35,937 --> 00:27:38,962
That's twenty four bits of relative precision.

487
00:27:38,982 --> 00:27:41,611
The register x must come back unchanged,

488
00:27:41,631 --> 00:27:44,524
and every scratch qubit must return to zero.

489
00:27:45,665 --> 00:27:50,156
How do you apply a phase that's stored as a number in a register?

490
00:27:50,176 --> 00:27:55,778
If y is a binary fraction, then e to the two pi i y is a product of one phase per

491
00:27:55,798 --> 00:27:56,056
bit.

492
00:27:56,076 --> 00:27:59,179
So you apply one Z POW gate to each bit of y,

493
00:27:59,199 --> 00:28:02,094
with an angle that halves from bit to bit.

494
00:28:02,431 --> 00:28:06,005
The biggest angles are Clifford gates, which are free.

495
00:28:06,025 --> 00:28:08,202
The eighth of a turn is a T gate.

496
00:28:08,222 --> 00:28:11,862
Everything finer is a rotation, and costs real T gates.

497
00:28:13,031 --> 00:28:17,955
The reference circuit borrows an idea made famous by the video game Quake Three:

498
00:28:17,975 --> 00:28:19,747
the fast inverse square root.

499
00:28:19,767 --> 00:28:24,382
Make a cheap first guess, then improve it with one step of Newton's method.

500
00:28:24,402 --> 00:28:27,966
The quantum version works in fixed point, in three stages.

501
00:28:29,131 --> 00:28:29,774
Stage one:

502
00:28:29,794 --> 00:28:30,437
normalize.

503
00:28:30,457 --> 00:28:35,606
Count the pairs of leading zeros, call that s, and shift x left by two s bits.

504
00:28:35,626 --> 00:28:39,383
Now the number sits between one and four, whatever x was.

505
00:28:39,403 --> 00:28:44,618
And since dividing by the square root of four to the s is the same as halving s

506
00:28:44,638 --> 00:28:48,992
times, the shift just turns into a shift of the answer at the end.

507
00:28:50,131 --> 00:28:50,799
Stage two:

508
00:28:50,819 --> 00:28:52,175
a cheap first guess.

509
00:28:52,195 --> 00:28:57,539
A cubic polynomial, with coefficients chosen by the top bits of the normalized

510
00:28:57,559 --> 00:29:00,015
input, approximates one over root x.

511
00:29:00,035 --> 00:29:03,041
The two curves are nearly indistinguishable:

512
00:29:03,061 --> 00:29:05,860
the guess is good to about fourteen bits.

513
00:29:06,998 --> 00:29:07,757
Stage three:

514
00:29:07,777 --> 00:29:09,575
one step of Newton's method.

515
00:29:09,595 --> 00:29:13,470
Each Newton step roughly doubles the number of correct bits.

516
00:29:13,490 --> 00:29:16,067
Fourteen bits become about twenty seven,

517
00:29:16,087 --> 00:29:19,118
comfortably more than the twenty four required.

518
00:29:19,138 --> 00:29:21,130
It costs three multiplications.

519
00:29:22,298 --> 00:29:26,415
Then shift the result by s, apply one Z POW gate per bit,

520
00:29:26,435 --> 00:29:29,391
and run everything backwards to clean up.

521
00:29:29,731 --> 00:29:34,570
The reference circuit uses two hundred sixty qubits and seven thousand seven

522
00:29:34,590 --> 00:29:39,237
hundred thirty five Toffolis, plus one T gate and thirty seven rotations.

523
00:29:39,257 --> 00:29:42,626
The cost per shot is Toffolis plus T gates over four,

524
00:29:42,646 --> 00:29:45,631
about seven thousand nine hundred eighty eight.

525
00:29:45,651 --> 00:29:47,868
And the score is qubits times that:

526
00:29:47,888 --> 00:29:49,978
about two point oh eight million.

527
00:29:51,131 --> 00:29:52,896
One subtlety in the pricing.

528
00:29:52,916 --> 00:29:56,082
The rotation precision isn't something you choose.

529
00:29:56,102 --> 00:29:59,588
The verifier measures your worst-case arithmetic error,

530
00:29:59,608 --> 00:30:02,519
takes the leftover margin under the tolerance,

531
00:30:02,539 --> 00:30:05,515
and splits it evenly across all your rotations.

532
00:30:05,535 --> 00:30:09,147
So more accurate arithmetic makes every rotation cheaper,

533
00:30:09,167 --> 00:30:12,780
and fewer rotations each get a bigger share of the slack.

534
00:30:13,931 --> 00:30:15,179
Where are the points?

535
00:30:15,199 --> 00:30:19,887
Within days of launch, public notebooks had pushed this to about seventy eight

536
00:30:19,907 --> 00:30:23,207
qubits and under seventeen hundred Toffoli equivalents:

537
00:30:23,227 --> 00:30:26,164
roughly sixteen times cheaper than the reference.

538
00:30:26,184 --> 00:30:30,510
The area of the green box, compared to the red one, is that improvement.

539
00:30:31,665 --> 00:30:33,463
The ideas behind those gains:

540
00:30:33,483 --> 00:30:37,665
replace Newton's method with a table lookup and a short polynomial.

541
00:30:37,685 --> 00:30:42,180
Use signed digits, so each step of the polynomial is a plain addition or

542
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:44,689
subtraction instead of a multiplication.

543
00:30:44,709 --> 00:30:48,264
Keep every register as narrow as the error budget allows.

544
00:30:48,284 --> 00:30:50,270
Erase everything by measurement.

545
00:30:50,290 --> 00:30:53,343
And remember that qubits multiply the whole cost:

546
00:30:53,363 --> 00:30:58,235
at around eighty qubits, each qubit you remove is worth more than one percent.

547
00:30:58,598 --> 00:30:59,817
And a practical tip.

548
00:30:59,837 --> 00:31:04,338
Before you build any circuit, write a bit-exact model of your fixed-point

549
00:31:04,358 --> 00:31:05,576
arithmetic in NumPy.

550
00:31:05,596 --> 00:31:09,849
You can check your error on all three million inputs in milliseconds,

551
00:31:09,869 --> 00:31:12,884
and only build the circuit once the numbers work.

552
00:31:18,031 --> 00:31:23,167
Benchmark two is the most mathematical of the three, so let's take it slowly.

553
00:31:23,187 --> 00:31:28,189
It comes from an algorithm called decoded quantum interferometry, or D Q I,

554
00:31:28,209 --> 00:31:31,939
proposed by researchers at Google in twenty twenty four.

555
00:31:31,959 --> 00:31:36,961
It's one of the most promising candidates for a useful quantum advantage in

556
00:31:36,981 --> 00:31:37,831
optimization.

557
00:31:38,997 --> 00:31:43,411
The optimization problem is called optimal polynomial intersection.

558
00:31:43,431 --> 00:31:45,397
You're given a list of points.

559
00:31:45,417 --> 00:31:49,632
At each point there's a set of allowed values, the blue squares.

560
00:31:49,652 --> 00:31:50,228
The goal:

561
00:31:50,248 --> 00:31:55,721
find a polynomial of low degree whose value lands inside the allowed set at as many

562
00:31:55,741 --> 00:31:56,978
points as possible.

563
00:31:57,331 --> 00:31:59,240
Checking a candidate is easy:

564
00:31:59,260 --> 00:32:02,101
evaluate the polynomial and count the hits.

565
00:32:02,121 --> 00:32:07,490
Finding a really good one is believed to be hard for classical computers at these

566
00:32:07,510 --> 00:32:07,889
sizes.

567
00:32:07,909 --> 00:32:09,752
Easy to check, hard to find:

568
00:32:09,772 --> 00:32:14,143
that's what makes it a candidate for verifiable quantum advantage.

569
00:32:15,297 --> 00:32:17,680
Everything happens in a finite field.

570
00:32:17,700 --> 00:32:22,679
Here, G F two to the twelve, which has four thousand and ninety six elements.

571
00:32:22,699 --> 00:32:28,003
Each element is a twelve-bit string, thought of as a polynomial in a symbol alpha,

572
00:32:28,023 --> 00:32:29,691
with bits as coefficients.

573
00:32:30,031 --> 00:32:34,379
Adding two elements is just XOR, bit by bit, with no carries.

574
00:32:34,399 --> 00:32:36,671
In a circuit, that's CNOT gates.

575
00:32:36,691 --> 00:32:37,029
Free.

576
00:32:37,997 --> 00:32:40,912
Multiplication is polynomial multiplication,

577
00:32:40,932 --> 00:32:44,313
then reduction by a fixed degree-twelve polynomial.

578
00:32:44,333 --> 00:32:48,781
Multiplying by a known constant is linear, so it's also just CNOTs.

579
00:32:48,801 --> 00:32:52,049
Multiplying two quantum registers costs Toffolis:

580
00:32:52,069 --> 00:32:56,051
sixty three in the reference circuit's Karatsuba multiplier.

581
00:32:56,071 --> 00:32:59,252
And inversion, one over a, is the expensive one:

582
00:32:59,272 --> 00:33:03,587
three hundred and fifteen Toffolis, using the Itoh Tsujii method.

583
00:33:04,731 --> 00:33:06,323
Now, Reed Solomon codes.

584
00:33:06,343 --> 00:33:10,353
They're used everywhere, from QR codes to deep-space probes.

585
00:33:10,373 --> 00:33:15,188
Take a message, treat it as the coefficients of a low-degree polynomial,

586
00:33:15,208 --> 00:33:18,480
and store the polynomial's values at many points.

587
00:33:18,500 --> 00:33:22,980
A low-degree polynomial is pinned down by just a few of its values,

588
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,330
so the extra values are redundancy.

589
00:33:25,697 --> 00:33:30,648
If a few stored values get corrupted, the redundancy lets you find and fix them.

590
00:33:30,997 --> 00:33:33,053
The tool for that is the syndrome:

591
00:33:33,073 --> 00:33:35,067
a short fingerprint of the error,

592
00:33:35,087 --> 00:33:38,362
computed from the received word by a fixed linear map.

593
00:33:38,382 --> 00:33:40,987
It's zero exactly when there are no errors,

594
00:33:41,007 --> 00:33:44,649
and different small error patterns give different syndromes.

595
00:33:44,669 --> 00:33:45,564
Decoding means:

596
00:33:45,584 --> 00:33:47,945
given the syndrome, work out the error.

597
00:33:49,097 --> 00:33:52,042
Here's D Q I's big idea, at a high level.

598
00:33:52,062 --> 00:33:57,537
The quantum Fourier transform turns the optimization problem into a decoding

599
00:33:57,557 --> 00:33:58,115
problem.

600
00:33:58,135 --> 00:34:02,309
D Q I prepares a superposition of sparse error patterns e,

601
00:34:02,329 --> 00:34:04,406
and computes their syndromes.

602
00:34:04,764 --> 00:34:07,895
Then it must erase e, keeping only the syndrome.

603
00:34:07,915 --> 00:34:12,620
But erasing e without looking at it means computing e from its syndrome.

604
00:34:12,640 --> 00:34:13,670
That's decoding:

605
00:34:13,690 --> 00:34:15,902
done reversibly, in superposition.

606
00:34:15,922 --> 00:34:20,759
Finally, a Fourier transform produces a state whose measurement gives good

607
00:34:20,779 --> 00:34:21,547
polynomials.

608
00:34:21,567 --> 00:34:26,076
The decoder is by far the most expensive part, so it's the benchmark.

609
00:34:27,231 --> 00:34:28,243
There's a twist.

610
00:34:28,263 --> 00:34:33,471
The improved construction erases the error symbols one at a time, by measurement:

611
00:34:33,491 --> 00:34:36,568
our old friend, measurement-based uncomputation.

612
00:34:36,588 --> 00:34:39,020
Each measurement leaves behind a sign:

613
00:34:39,040 --> 00:34:44,119
minus one to the c dot e, where c is the random record of measurement outcomes.

614
00:34:44,139 --> 00:34:48,313
Those signs must be cancelled before the final Fourier transform.

615
00:34:48,333 --> 00:34:52,637
And cancelling them still requires knowing e, which means decoding.

616
00:34:52,997 --> 00:34:54,482
So here's the benchmark.

617
00:34:54,502 --> 00:34:58,809
Your circuit receives a syndrome s in eight hundred and forty qubits,

618
00:34:58,829 --> 00:35:02,508
and a classical record c of about forty nine thousand bits.

619
00:35:02,528 --> 00:35:05,894
It must apply the sign, minus one to the c dot e of s,

620
00:35:05,914 --> 00:35:11,036
where e of s is the unique error of weight at most thirty five with that syndrome.

621
00:35:11,056 --> 00:35:14,798
It must leave s unchanged, and return all workspace to zero.

622
00:35:14,818 --> 00:35:17,244
The error itself is never given to you.

623
00:35:17,264 --> 00:35:19,000
Your circuit has to find it.

624
00:35:20,164 --> 00:35:20,943
The numbers:

625
00:35:20,963 --> 00:35:25,339
four thousand and ninety five positions, seventy syndrome symbols,

626
00:35:25,359 --> 00:35:29,468
the field G F two to the twelve, and up to thirty five errors.

627
00:35:29,488 --> 00:35:32,265
The code has minimum distance seventy one,

628
00:35:32,285 --> 00:35:36,194
which is why any thirty five errors give a unique syndrome.

629
00:35:37,364 --> 00:35:40,008
The reference decoder has three stages.

630
00:35:40,028 --> 00:35:45,335
First, solve the so-called key equation with the extended Euclidean algorithm,

631
00:35:45,355 --> 00:35:46,633
run on polynomials.

632
00:35:46,653 --> 00:35:48,477
That gives two polynomials:

633
00:35:48,497 --> 00:35:52,848
the error locator, sigma, whose roots mark where the errors are,

634
00:35:52,868 --> 00:35:54,965
and the error evaluator, omega.

635
00:35:55,331 --> 00:35:56,882
Second, a Chien search:

636
00:35:56,902 --> 00:36:01,116
evaluate sigma at all four thousand and ninety five positions.

637
00:36:01,136 --> 00:36:02,619
A zero marks an error.

638
00:36:02,639 --> 00:36:06,649
There, Forney's formula gives the error value, and Z gates,

639
00:36:06,669 --> 00:36:09,517
chosen by the record bits, apply the sign.

640
00:36:09,537 --> 00:36:15,186
Third, run the Euclidean algorithm backwards, to restore the syndrome and clean up.

641
00:36:16,331 --> 00:36:20,376
The reference costs one thousand eight hundred ninety three qubits,

642
00:36:20,396 --> 00:36:23,046
and about five point seven million Toffolis.

643
00:36:23,066 --> 00:36:27,840
A lot of that goes on doing a field inversion at every one of the four thousand

644
00:36:27,860 --> 00:36:29,175
ninety five positions.

645
00:36:29,531 --> 00:36:33,831
The cost formula is R of epsilon, times Q squared, times T.

646
00:36:33,851 --> 00:36:39,689
Qubits are squared, so cutting qubits by one percent is worth as much as cutting

647
00:36:39,709 --> 00:36:41,447
Toffolis by two percent.

648
00:36:42,597 --> 00:36:44,295
R is a repetition penalty.

649
00:36:44,315 --> 00:36:46,474
Approximate decoders are allowed:

650
00:36:46,494 --> 00:36:51,031
if your decoder fails sometimes, D Q I just has to be run more times.

651
00:36:51,051 --> 00:36:55,721
The verifier tests one hundred and two thousand four hundred syndromes,

652
00:36:55,741 --> 00:36:59,684
with errors drawn the way D Q I would actually produce them,

653
00:36:59,704 --> 00:37:03,911
and turns the failure count into a certified bound epsilon plus.

654
00:37:04,264 --> 00:37:08,079
Even a perfect decoder pays R of about one point one nine,

655
00:37:08,099 --> 00:37:12,244
because a hundred thousand shots can't certify anything better.

656
00:37:12,264 --> 00:37:15,219
One percent failures nearly doubles the cost.

657
00:37:15,239 --> 00:37:19,384
And failing more than one time in sixteen gets no score at all.

658
00:37:20,531 --> 00:37:21,729
Where are the points?

659
00:37:21,749 --> 00:37:26,486
Public notebooks report around eight hundred and eighty qubits and under a million

660
00:37:26,506 --> 00:37:27,008
Toffolis.

661
00:37:27,028 --> 00:37:30,546
That's roughly twenty seven times cheaper than the reference,

662
00:37:30,566 --> 00:37:32,460
and the leaders are higher still.

663
00:37:32,797 --> 00:37:33,408
The ideas:

664
00:37:33,428 --> 00:37:35,302
work almost entirely in place,

665
00:37:35,322 --> 00:37:39,404
reusing the eight hundred and forty syndrome qubits as workspace.

666
00:37:39,424 --> 00:37:44,200
Do fewer field inversions, by sharing them, batching them, or avoiding them.

667
00:37:44,220 --> 00:37:48,491
Use cheaper multipliers and smarter bookkeeping for the polynomials.

668
00:37:48,511 --> 00:37:50,511
And design for the typical case:

669
00:37:50,531 --> 00:37:53,982
most error patterns have fewer than thirty five errors,

670
00:37:54,002 --> 00:37:57,516
and rare failures are allowed, as long as R stays small.

671
00:38:02,664 --> 00:38:05,055
Benchmark three is quantum chemistry.

672
00:38:05,075 --> 00:38:10,202
The target is FeMoCo, the iron molybdenum cofactor at the heart of nitrogenase.

673
00:38:10,222 --> 00:38:15,023
That's the enzyme bacteria use to turn nitrogen from the air into ammonia,

674
00:38:15,043 --> 00:38:16,326
at room temperature.

675
00:38:16,346 --> 00:38:19,975
Industry does the same job with the Haber Bosch process,

676
00:38:19,995 --> 00:38:24,600
which consumes something like one to two percent of the world's energy.

677
00:38:24,620 --> 00:38:29,161
Understanding FeMoCo is one of the famous goals for quantum computers.

678
00:38:30,331 --> 00:38:32,351
We want its ground state energy.

679
00:38:32,371 --> 00:38:37,195
The model uses seventy six orbitals, and one hundred and thirteen electrons.

680
00:38:37,215 --> 00:38:42,423
The Hamiltonian is defined by one-body integrals, with about six thousand numbers,

681
00:38:42,443 --> 00:38:45,929
and two-body integrals, with seventy six to the fourth:

682
00:38:45,949 --> 00:38:48,160
about thirty three million numbers.

683
00:38:49,297 --> 00:38:52,759
Energies are estimated with quantum phase estimation,

684
00:38:52,779 --> 00:38:54,926
applied to a block encoding of H.

685
00:38:54,946 --> 00:38:59,722
That's a unitary circuit that holds H, divided by a normalization lambda,

686
00:38:59,742 --> 00:39:01,889
in one corner of a bigger matrix.

687
00:39:01,909 --> 00:39:07,276
The algorithm calls this circuit a number of times proportional to lambda over the

688
00:39:07,296 --> 00:39:08,458
accuracy you want.

689
00:39:08,478 --> 00:39:11,414
So a smaller lambda means a faster algorithm,

690
00:39:11,434 --> 00:39:14,633
and a cheaper circuit means each call costs less.

691
00:39:15,797 --> 00:39:19,934
The standard construction is a linear combination of unitaries.

692
00:39:19,954 --> 00:39:22,771
Write H as a weighted sum of simple pieces.

693
00:39:22,791 --> 00:39:27,323
Prepare a state whose amplitudes are the square roots of the weights.

694
00:39:27,343 --> 00:39:29,699
Apply the piece chosen by the index.

695
00:39:29,719 --> 00:39:30,688
Then unprepare.

696
00:39:30,708 --> 00:39:32,865
Lambda is the sum of the weights.

697
00:39:34,031 --> 00:39:38,416
Feeding thirty three million numbers into a circuit would be hopeless.

698
00:39:38,436 --> 00:39:42,506
So the two-body tensor is compressed with a tensor factorization.

699
00:39:42,526 --> 00:39:47,226
You choose R groups, each with B unit vectors u, in seventy six dimensions,

700
00:39:47,246 --> 00:39:49,302
plus C sets of weights per group.

701
00:39:49,322 --> 00:39:53,141
The tensor is rebuilt from sums of products of these vectors.

702
00:39:53,497 --> 00:39:58,786
The factorization must reproduce the true integrals to within twenty milli-hartree,

703
00:39:58,806 --> 00:40:00,769
measured in the Frobenius norm.

704
00:40:00,789 --> 00:40:04,926
The provided baseline uses sixty five groups, sixty vectors each,

705
00:40:04,946 --> 00:40:06,461
and sixty weight copies.

706
00:40:06,481 --> 00:40:09,659
This part should feel familiar to an A I engineer:

707
00:40:09,679 --> 00:40:14,072
it's fitting a low-rank model to a big tensor, with gradient descent,

708
00:40:14,092 --> 00:40:15,223
under constraints.

709
00:40:16,364 --> 00:40:19,128
Now, how does the circuit use a unit vector?

710
00:40:19,148 --> 00:40:21,975
Through Givens rotations, arranged on a tree.

711
00:40:21,995 --> 00:40:25,645
Picture a tree over the orbitals, here just eight of them.

712
00:40:25,665 --> 00:40:29,062
Start with all of the vector's weight on orbital zero.

713
00:40:29,431 --> 00:40:32,836
Each edge of the tree is a rotation by an angle theta,

714
00:40:32,856 --> 00:40:36,578
that moves some of the weight from the parent to the child.

715
00:40:36,598 --> 00:40:39,813
The child gets the parent's value times sine theta,

716
00:40:39,833 --> 00:40:41,970
and the parent keeps cosine theta.

717
00:40:42,331 --> 00:40:46,996
Seventy six orbitals means seventy five edges, so seventy five angles,

718
00:40:47,016 --> 00:40:49,941
and with them you can reach any unit vector.

719
00:40:49,961 --> 00:40:54,426
On the quantum side, each edge becomes a fermionic Givens rotation,

720
00:40:54,446 --> 00:40:56,835
controlled by the bits of its angle.

721
00:40:57,997 --> 00:41:00,669
And this is the circuit you actually build:

722
00:41:00,689 --> 00:41:01,733
the angle loader.

723
00:41:01,753 --> 00:41:05,112
Given the address of a vector in a selection register,

724
00:41:05,132 --> 00:41:08,304
it writes that vector's angles into word registers.

725
00:41:08,324 --> 00:41:10,057
It's a lookup table, a QROM.

726
00:41:10,077 --> 00:41:14,938
With the baseline factorization, there are seventy six plus R times B entries:

727
00:41:14,958 --> 00:41:19,883
three thousand nine hundred seventy six vectors, with seventy five angles each,

728
00:41:19,903 --> 00:41:21,760
every angle a twelve-bit word.

729
00:41:22,897 --> 00:41:26,613
The seventy five angles are streamed in batches of J words.

730
00:41:26,633 --> 00:41:31,107
Circuit k XORs in the difference between batch k and batch k minus one.

731
00:41:31,127 --> 00:41:34,779
So the same J registers step from batch to batch in place,

732
00:41:34,799 --> 00:41:37,628
and running the family backwards clears them.

733
00:41:37,648 --> 00:41:42,376
Each of these circuits may use at most one hundred and twenty eight qubits.

734
00:41:43,531 --> 00:41:47,983
The score is lambda effective, times total Toffolis, times total qubits.

735
00:41:48,003 --> 00:41:52,518
The Toffoli count includes your angle circuits, run forward and backward,

736
00:41:52,538 --> 00:41:55,935
plus formula-based costs for the parts you don't build:

737
00:41:55,955 --> 00:41:58,978
the state preparations, and the Givens rotations,

738
00:41:58,998 --> 00:42:01,898
whose cost grows with the number of angle bits.

739
00:42:01,918 --> 00:42:05,936
Total qubits counts the persistent registers, the word registers,

740
00:42:05,956 --> 00:42:08,172
and the largest temporary workspace.

741
00:42:08,531 --> 00:42:12,626
Lambda effective comes from a trick called spectrum amplification.

742
00:42:12,646 --> 00:42:15,744
It combines the raw normalization, capital lambda,

743
00:42:15,764 --> 00:42:20,795
with an energy gap that measures how far the factorization's sum-of-squares lower

744
00:42:20,815 --> 00:42:23,664
bound sits below the true ground state energy.

745
00:42:23,684 --> 00:42:24,786
Smaller is better.

746
00:42:24,806 --> 00:42:28,091
And to stop degenerate factorizations from gaming it,

747
00:42:28,111 --> 00:42:30,710
capital lambda must stay above forty nine,

748
00:42:30,730 --> 00:42:33,641
and the gap above three point nine one hartree.

749
00:42:34,797 --> 00:42:35,645
The baseline:

750
00:42:35,665 --> 00:42:40,851
lambda effective forty nine point three, about ninety three thousand Toffolis,

751
00:42:40,871 --> 00:42:42,720
seven hundred thirty qubits.

752
00:42:42,740 --> 00:42:45,857
A score of about three point three six billion.

753
00:42:46,197 --> 00:42:49,582
A public notebook already does about four times better,

754
00:42:49,602 --> 00:42:51,872
mostly by changing the factorization:

755
00:42:51,892 --> 00:42:54,967
a single group of four hundred ninety six vectors.

756
00:42:54,987 --> 00:42:59,547
That's only five hundred seventy two lookup entries instead of almost four

757
00:42:59,567 --> 00:43:04,499
thousand, and the Toffoli count drops from ninety three thousand to about twenty

758
00:43:04,519 --> 00:43:05,056
thousand.

759
00:43:06,197 --> 00:43:08,500
So this task has levers on both sides.

760
00:43:08,520 --> 00:43:09,233
Classically:

761
00:43:09,253 --> 00:43:12,900
fewer vectors, a smaller lambda effective, fewer angle bits,

762
00:43:12,920 --> 00:43:16,262
all while keeping the error under twenty milli-hartree.

763
00:43:16,282 --> 00:43:17,484
On the quantum side:

764
00:43:17,504 --> 00:43:21,946
cheaper lookups, since a lookup's cost scales with the number of entries.

765
00:43:21,966 --> 00:43:26,591
The batch size, the tree shape, and the word layout are yours to choose too.

766
00:43:26,931 --> 00:43:32,378
Of the three benchmarks, this one rewards an A I engineer's skills most directly:

767
00:43:32,398 --> 00:43:35,213
a constrained, non-convex fitting problem,

768
00:43:35,233 --> 00:43:38,115
with a cost model you can optimize against.

769
00:43:42,664 --> 00:43:44,570
Let's put it all together.

770
00:43:44,590 --> 00:43:47,015
Three tasks, three cost formulas.

771
00:43:47,035 --> 00:43:48,052
Benchmark one:

772
00:43:48,072 --> 00:43:51,385
qubits times Toffolis plus T gates over four.

773
00:43:51,405 --> 00:43:52,422
Benchmark two:

774
00:43:52,442 --> 00:43:56,941
the repetition penalty, times qubits squared, times Toffolis.

775
00:43:56,961 --> 00:43:58,126
Benchmark three:

776
00:43:58,146 --> 00:44:02,496
lambda effective, times total Toffolis, times total qubits.

777
00:44:02,864 --> 00:44:07,000
Your score is the geometric mean of the three improvement ratios.

778
00:44:07,020 --> 00:44:12,307
Since it's a geometric mean, a task you leave at the reference contributes a factor

779
00:44:12,327 --> 00:44:17,103
of one, and doubling any single ratio raises your score by the same amount,

780
00:44:17,123 --> 00:44:18,701
about twenty six percent.

781
00:44:19,864 --> 00:44:22,760
A submission is a zip file with your circuits:

782
00:44:22,780 --> 00:44:26,881
one binary kickmix file for benchmark one, one for benchmark two,

783
00:44:26,901 --> 00:44:31,953
and a folder for benchmark three, with the angle circuits and the factorization.

784
00:44:31,973 --> 00:44:36,834
It mirrors the sample submission folder that ships with the competition data.

785
00:44:36,854 --> 00:44:40,638
Check the competition's evaluation page for the exact rules;

786
00:44:40,658 --> 00:44:43,110
you only get a few submissions per day.

787
00:44:44,264 --> 00:44:47,476
The competition data gives you everything you need:

788
00:44:47,496 --> 00:44:51,785
kickmix itself, the three official verifiers with their source code,

789
00:44:51,805 --> 00:44:56,601
the reference circuit generators, the FeMoCo integrals, a sample submission,

790
00:44:56,621 --> 00:44:58,122
and the companion paper.

791
00:44:58,142 --> 00:44:59,452
Your very first step:

792
00:44:59,472 --> 00:45:02,747
run the verifiers on the sample submission yourself,

793
00:45:02,767 --> 00:45:04,902
and confirm it scores exactly one.

794
00:45:06,064 --> 00:45:07,943
Then the workflow is a loop.

795
00:45:07,963 --> 00:45:12,962
Write a Python program that generates your circuit with kickmix's builder.

796
00:45:12,982 --> 00:45:13,573
Build it.

797
00:45:13,593 --> 00:45:16,625
Verify it locally with the official verifier.

798
00:45:16,645 --> 00:45:22,254
Read the cost breakdown, kickmix even draws flame charts of where your Toffolis and

799
00:45:22,274 --> 00:45:22,933
qubits go.

800
00:45:22,953 --> 00:45:24,018
Change one idea.

801
00:45:24,038 --> 00:45:24,493
Repeat.

802
00:45:24,831 --> 00:45:29,657
And this is exactly the loop the organizers designed for A I coding agents:

803
00:45:29,677 --> 00:45:35,019
propose a change, build, verify, score, keep the best, and repeat, many times over.

804
00:45:35,039 --> 00:45:37,345
The organizers ran their own agents,

805
00:45:37,365 --> 00:45:41,739
published the SHA-256 hashes of their best circuits as a commitment,

806
00:45:41,759 --> 00:45:45,357
and say that all three benchmarks can still be improved.

807
00:45:45,377 --> 00:45:49,751
Evolutionary search with coding agents, in the style of AlphaEvolve,

808
00:45:49,771 --> 00:45:50,849
is a natural fit.

809
00:45:51,997 --> 00:45:55,217
Here's a concrete plan for your first two weeks.

810
00:45:55,237 --> 00:45:55,487
One:

811
00:45:55,507 --> 00:46:00,753
verify the sample submission, and read the source code of the three verifiers.

812
00:46:00,773 --> 00:46:01,023
Two:

813
00:46:01,043 --> 00:46:03,115
build tiny circuits in kickmix:

814
00:46:03,135 --> 00:46:05,950
a temporary AND, an adder, a small lookup.

815
00:46:05,970 --> 00:46:06,895
Simulate them.

816
00:46:06,915 --> 00:46:10,608
Break them on purpose, and watch the verifier catch it.

817
00:46:10,964 --> 00:46:11,331
Three:

818
00:46:11,351 --> 00:46:12,945
start with benchmark one.

819
00:46:12,965 --> 00:46:16,043
It has a single register, every input is tested,

820
00:46:16,063 --> 00:46:18,754
and you can model the arithmetic in NumPy.

821
00:46:18,774 --> 00:46:19,076
Four:

822
00:46:19,096 --> 00:46:20,690
try benchmark three next.

823
00:46:20,710 --> 00:46:25,853
The factorization is a fitting problem, right in an A I engineer's comfort zone.

824
00:46:25,873 --> 00:46:26,176
Five:

825
00:46:26,196 --> 00:46:28,048
leave benchmark two for last.

826
00:46:28,068 --> 00:46:31,404
It rewards depth in finite fields and coding theory.

827
00:46:31,424 --> 00:46:31,920
And six:

828
00:46:31,940 --> 00:46:36,503
read the public notebooks, and reproduce one before you try to beat it.

829
00:46:37,664 --> 00:46:38,832
And a reading list.

830
00:46:38,852 --> 00:46:41,083
First, the competition paper itself:

831
00:46:41,103 --> 00:46:42,333
now you can read it.

832
00:46:42,353 --> 00:46:46,459
Then Craig Gidney's paper on halving the cost of quantum addition.

833
00:46:46,479 --> 00:46:51,336
Babbush and coauthors on encoding electronic spectra with linear T complexity,

834
00:46:51,356 --> 00:46:55,212
which introduced the lookup and unary iteration constructions.

835
00:46:55,232 --> 00:46:58,588
Low, Kliuchnikov and Schaeffer on select-swap lookups.

836
00:46:58,608 --> 00:47:01,714
The original decoded quantum interferometry paper.

837
00:47:01,734 --> 00:47:04,215
And, for the basics, Nielsen and Chuang.

838
00:47:05,364 --> 00:47:08,726
Remember the words from the start of this video?

839
00:47:08,746 --> 00:47:09,289
Toffoli.

840
00:47:09,309 --> 00:47:10,276
Uncomputation.

841
00:47:10,296 --> 00:47:10,769
Hammer.

842
00:47:10,789 --> 00:47:11,192
Q-rom.

843
00:47:11,212 --> 00:47:11,826
Syndrome.

844
00:47:11,846 --> 00:47:12,882
Block encoding.

845
00:47:12,902 --> 00:47:15,348
They should all mean something now.

846
00:47:16,497 --> 00:47:16,667
So:

847
00:47:16,687 --> 00:47:20,910
could we get more from a quantum computer, by asking it to do less?

848
00:47:20,930 --> 00:47:24,139
Every Toffoli you remove, and every qubit you save,

849
00:47:24,159 --> 00:47:27,432
is a little less work for a future quantum computer,

850
00:47:27,452 --> 00:47:30,725
and brings useful quantum computing a little closer.

851
00:47:30,745 --> 00:47:31,358
Good luck.
